course: dictionary
lesson: 14142
title: Misconduct: bad behaviour at work
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Misconduct means bad or wrong behaviour, especially by someone in a responsible job who breaks the rules. Look for clues: a job, rules, and a person who should know better.
explain: «The bank clerk was sacked for misconduct after he took money from customers.» The clues are sacked, bank and took money, so misconduct must mean seriously wrong behaviour at work. Check it: put in "bad behaviour" instead, and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Gross misconduct is even more serious. «Gross misconduct, such as stealing or violence at work, can get someone sacked at once.» Here gross means very serious, not disgusting, and at once means straight away.
fact: Misconduct is a noun, and it is uncountable: say «misconduct», not «a misconduct».
fact: «Misconduct» means bad or wrong behaviour, especially by someone in a responsible job who breaks the rules.
fact: «Gross misconduct» means very serious wrong behaviour, such as stealing or violence at work.
fact: Common phrases include «professional misconduct», «accused of misconduct» and «sacked for misconduct».
fact: «Redundancy» is different: a job ends because it is no longer needed, and this is not the worker's fault.
exercise: «The manager was sacked for misconduct after stealing money from the shop.» What does misconduct mean? | A kind of shop | *Bad behaviour at work, breaking the rules | Losing a job because the company is closing | Being late for work once
exercise: What does «gross misconduct» mean? | A small mistake in a sum | Very good work | A spelling error in a letter | *Very serious wrong behaviour
exercise: Find and copy the word in «The footballer was fined for misconduct on the pitch» that names the bad behaviour. | pitch | *misconduct | footballer | fined
test: Which sentence uses misconduct correctly? | The worker was misconduct sacked. | *The worker was sacked for misconduct. | The worker was sacked for a misconducts. | The worker was sacked by misconduct.
test: Why might a worker be sacked for misconduct rather than made redundant? | Because the company had no more work | *Because the worker broke the rules, and it was their fault | Because the worker was ill for a week | Because the worker moved house
exam: «Misconduct» most nearly means which? | A sad feeling | A kind of job | Good manners | *Bad or wrong behaviour
